<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="section-function-examples" >
<title>Methods for Infinity</title>

<introduction>

<p>Calculus is concerned with addressing problems that involve some kind of infinity. In this first activity we will work with two examples where a pattern in slopes requires us to consider infinitely many cases. The first case is typical of what traditional high school algebra courses consider. It derives from the question, "What is the slope of a single point?"</p>

<p>The second is a function for which there is no formula, though we can precisely describe it. This example will be used throughout the semester to answer questions about what is possible.</p>

</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-function-slope-examples">
    <title>Finding the Slope of One Point</title>

<activity xml:id="activity-polynomial-slope-one-point">
    <title>Making Sense of the Slope of One Point</title>
    <introduction><p>The goals of this activity are to see what can happen with sets of nearby slopes and to become comfortable working with functions that have no formula.</p></introduction>
    <task><introduction><p>First we will consider the polynomial <m>y=2x^3-9x^2+10x-3</m>.</p></introduction>
        <task><p>Calculate the points on this polynomial at <m>x=1</m> and <m>x=2</m>.</p></task>
        <task><p>Find the slope of the line between these two points.</p></task>
        <task><p>Repeat this process to complete <xref ref="table-example-slopes-polynomial" />. Note technology is great for this task.</p></task>
        <task><p>Do you notice any pattern in the slopes?</p></task>
        <task><p>Based on this is there a reasonable answer to the question what is the slope at the one point <m>x=1.5</m>?</p></task>
    </task>
    <task><introduction><p>Next we will consider the Cantor Function.</p></introduction>
        <task><introduction><p>Follow these directions to construct the graph of the Cantor function.</p></introduction>
            <task><statement><p>Construct the axes. The x-axis should be from 0 to 1. If using a ruler put 1 at 243 mm. The y-axis should be from 0 to 1. The actual height is not important here. Use a full sheet of paper.</p></statement></task>
            <task xml:id="activity-task-middle-third"><statement><p>Find the endpoints of the middle third along the x-axis.</p></statement></task>
            <task><statement><p>For the current segment what is the height of the raised segment immediately to its left? If there is no raised segment to the left use <m>y=0</m>. What is the height of the raised segment immediately to its right? If there is no raised segment to the right use <m>y=1</m>. What height is half way between these two heights?</p></statement></task>
            <task><statement><p>At this height (half-way between left and right) draw a segment over this middle third.</p></statement></task>
            <task><statement><p>Count the number of segments that you have.</p></statement></task>
            <task><statement><p>On each of the segments not yet raised repeat this process starting at <xref ref="activity-task-middle-third" />.</p></statement></task>
            <task><statement><p>Stop when you can no longer draw any smaller segments.</p></statement></task>
        </task>
        <task><introduction><p>Calculate slopes.</p></introduction>
            <task><statement><p>Calculate the points on the Cantor function at <m>x=\frac{1}{3}</m> and <m>x=\frac{2}{9}</m>.</p></statement></task>
            <task><statement><p>Find the slope of the line between these two points.</p></statement></task>
            <task><statement><p>Repeat this process to complete <xref ref="table-example-slopes-cantor" />.</p></statement></task>
            <task><statement><p>Do you notice any pattern in the slopes?</p></statement></task>
            <task><p>Based on this is there a reasonable answer to the question what is the slope at the one point <m>x=1/3</m>?</p></task>
        </task>
    </task>
</activity>

